## ponytail (concept credit)

The "minimal-code ladder" idea in skills/minimal-code/SKILL.md (ask whether
code is needed at all, whether it exists, whether the standard library or an
existing dependency covers it, whether one line suffices, and only then write
the minimum; never cut validation, security, accessibility or the one small
test) is adapted from the ponytail project
(<https://github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail>, MIT). Further concepts in the
same file and in skills/code-review/SKILL.md follow ponytail 5.3.3 (reviewed
2026-11-13): list what a change must reach before choosing the smallest
solution, fix a bug once in the shared code after checking every caller, no
test for a trivial change, a shortcut comment that names its limit and when to
upgrade, review findings with a concrete case and what happens if they are
skipped, a stated assumed load, and a closing "not checked" line. Applying
these ideas to verification, review rounds and dispatched agents is ytstack's
own extension. No ponytail text, code or assets are included; the prose is
ytstack's own.

## mattpocock/skills (concept credit)

Coordinator mode (skills/coordinate/, skills/finish-lane/,
skills/handover-step/, skills/lane-retro/, bin/ytstack-frontier,
bin/ytstack-questions and docs/ux/coordinator-format.md) adapts concepts from
the skill collection at <https://github.com/mattpocock/skills> (MIT, reviewed
at 4ae2317): a coordinating session whose memory outlives it and is written as
history, blocking edges and a frontier of ready work, a claim before any work,
a brief with checkable completion criteria, a staged script for steps only the
human may perform, a queue of decision-ready questions, and retros that end in
checks. No text, template or code from that collection is included; every
skill, script and template here is ytstack's own.

## Agentic software factory (concept credit)

The risk gate and the delivery flow (bin/ytstack-risk, the auto_merge ceiling
of an area, .ytstack/spec/REVIEW.md with its lenses, skills/visual-review/,
merge_by: risk, bin/ytstack-runtime and the "Run", "Release" and "Signals"
sections of RUNTIME.md, the release watch in bin/ytstack-watch,
bin/ytstack-upkeep, skills/signal-intake/ and skills/incident/) are inspired
by a published account of an agentic software factory, compared against
ytstack in docs/research/2026-11-13-openai-software-factory.md: a risk class
decided per change with a per-area permission to merge without a person,
second reviews by kind of change, the product startable per worktree and
inspected in a browser, a watched rollout, recurring upkeep runs, and an
incident helper that proposes mitigations a person orders. The floor that no
judgment can lower, the reviewer who is never the author, read-only access to
production and every mitigation as a staged script a person runs are
ytstack's own. No text, image or code from that account is included.

## External AI-first methodology

Conceptual influence (not vendored, not attributed by name per ytstack
design direction). We adopted the following concepts and re-implemented them
in our own prose:

- Three-tier context model (core / task / background)
- Component-based system thinking for AI organizations
- Skill-playbook-based skill creation
- Artifact-as-memory with append-only decision register

No source material, prose, or graphics from any external methodology is
included in this repository. All artifact formats, file structures, and
prose are original ytstack work.

